{
 "ep": 18,
 "total": 140,
 "title": "Roles vs Users: PostgreSQL Role Model",
 "phase": "Installation & Administration",
 "lines": [
  {
   "say": "PostgreSQL users and roles are not two separate systems.",
   "cap": "Users and roles are *not two systems.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "This is episode eighteen of the PostgreSQL DBA series. Today: the role model, LOGIN, membership, and inheritance.",
   "cap": "Episode 18: *roles,* *LOGIN* and *membership.*",
   "cue": "role"
  },
  {
   "say": "In PostgreSQL there is only one kind of account. It is called a role.",
   "cap": "Only one kind of account: a *role.*",
   "cue": "role"
  },
  {
   "say": "The docs say a role can act as a user, a group, or both. It depends on how you set it up.",
   "cap": "A role can be a *user,* a *group,* or *both.*",
   "cue": "group"
  },
  {
   "say": "So what is a user? Simply a role with the LOGIN attribute.",
   "cap": "A user is a role with *LOGIN.*",
   "cue": "LOGIN"
  },
  {
   "say": "CREATE USER is the same as CREATE ROLE, except that it adds LOGIN by default. Even its output says CREATE ROLE.",
   "cap": "*CREATE USER* = *CREATE ROLE* + LOGIN.",
   "cue": "output"
  },
  {
   "say": "Roles are global across the whole cluster, not per database. And they are separate from operating system users.",
   "cap": "Roles are *cluster-wide,* not per database.",
   "cue": "separate"
  },
  {
   "say": "A fresh cluster starts with one superuser role, often named postgres. A superuser bypasses every permission check, except the right to log in.",
   "cap": "*postgres* superuser bypasses *all* checks but login.",
   "cue": "bypasses"
  },
  {
   "say": "Let's test it. We create a group role called readers, and app_user with CREATE USER.",
   "cap": "Test: *readers* and *app_user.*",
   "cue": "app_user"
  },
  {
   "say": "The pg_roles view shows it: readers cannot log in, app_user can. Both inherit by default.",
   "cap": "*rolcanlogin:* readers f, app_user t.",
   "cue": "inherit"
  },
  {
   "say": "Connect as readers, and PostgreSQL refuses: role readers is not permitted to log in.",
   "cap": "*not permitted to log in*",
   "cue": "refuses"
  },
  {
   "say": "Now membership. We grant SELECT on orders to readers, then grant readers to app_user.",
   "cap": "Privileges go to the *group.* People join it.",
   "cue": "membership"
  },
  {
   "say": "app_user never got SELECT directly, yet it can read orders, because members inherit the group's privileges.",
   "cap": "app_user *inherits* SELECT from readers.",
   "cue": "inherit"
  },
  {
   "say": "In psql sixteen, backslash d r g lists role grants, with their options.",
   "cap": "*\\drg* lists role grants.",
   "cue": "options"
  },
  {
   "say": "Our grant shows two options: INHERIT and SET. Output trimmed.",
   "cap": "Options: *INHERIT* and *SET.*",
   "cue": "INHERIT"
  },
  {
   "say": "INHERIT means the group's privileges apply automatically. SET means the member may SET ROLE to become the group.",
   "cap": "*INHERIT:* automatic. *SET:* can become the group.",
   "cue": "SET"
  },
  {
   "say": "A common mistake: assuming attributes like CREATEDB flow down to members. The docs say they never do.",
   "cap": "Myth: *attributes* are inherited.",
   "cue": "never"
  },
  {
   "say": "Our test: app_user is a member of dbcreators, which has CREATEDB. Yet CREATE DATABASE fails with permission denied.",
   "cap": "Member of a CREATEDB role: *permission denied.*",
   "cue": "fails"
  },
  {
   "say": "After SET ROLE dbcreators, the same command works.",
   "cap": "*SET ROLE* first, then it works.",
   "cue": "works"
  },
  {
   "say": "LOGIN, SUPERUSER, CREATEDB and CREATEROLE are never inherited. To use them, you SET ROLE.",
   "cap": "Special attributes: *never inherited.*",
   "cue": "never"
  },
  {
   "say": "DBA takeaway, a common practice: least privilege.",
   "cap": "A common practice: *least privilege.*",
   "cue": "least"
  },
  {
   "say": "Grant privileges to group roles, and give login roles only the memberships they need.",
   "cap": "Grant to *groups,* not people.",
   "cue": "group"
  },
  {
   "say": "And the docs advise doing most of your work as a role that is not a superuser.",
   "cap": "Do daily work as a *non-superuser.*",
   "cue": "superuser"
  },
  {
   "say": "Recap: everything is a role, and a user is just a role with LOGIN. Roles are cluster-wide.",
   "cap": "Recap: *one role model,* LOGIN makes a user.",
   "cue": "everything"
  },
  {
   "say": "Members inherit privileges by default, but special attributes need SET ROLE.",
   "cap": "Privileges *inherit.* Attributes need *SET ROLE.*",
   "cue": "attributes"
  },
  {
   "say": "Next up: how to create a read-only user correctly. Follow so you don't miss it, and send this to a friend who's learning PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "Next up: *read-only users.* *Follow* and share."
  }
 ],
